{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{A}{(4-x)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(4-x)^{2}(2-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n9x-38=A(4-x)(2-x)+B(2-x)+C(4-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(4)-38 & =A(4-4)(2-4)+B(2-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 36-38 & =B(-2) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-2}{-2} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(2)-38 & =A(4-2)(2-2)+B(2-2)+C(4-2)^{2} \\\\ 18-38 & =C{(2)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{4} \\\\ C & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)-38 & =A(4-0)(2-0)+B(2-0)+C(4-0)^{2} \\\\ -38 & =8A+2B+16C \\\\ -38 & =8A+2(1)+16(-5) \\\\ -38 & =8A-78 \\\\ 8A & =78-38 \\\\ A & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|+\\frac{1}{4-x}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant.",
